# =============================================================================
# .dockerignore (PHASE 2, YOUR TURN)
# =============================================================================
# Works like .gitignore, but for "docker build". Anything listed here is NOT sent
# to Docker when it builds the image. This keeps builds fast and stops junk
# (or secrets) from ending up inside your image.
#
# Add patterns for:
#   - any build output folders (you don't want your Mac's compiled files inside a Linux image;
#     they won't even run there)
#   - checkpoint folders from local testing
#   - editor and OS junk files
#
# Then check it worked: build the image and look at the "transferring context" size
# in the output. It should be tiny (a few KB).
# =============================================================================
